## Comparison

| Method | Speed | Coverage | Effort |
|---|---|---|---|
| CLI (`az network nsg rule list`) | Instant | One NSG per command, so I must already know its name | Low |
| Resource Graph | `<fill in: seconds to minutes>` | Every NSG in every subscription in one query | Medium: needs the query |
| Defender for Cloud | `<fill in: minutes to hours>` | Broadest: flags more than NSG rules, but detail columns need a paid plan | Low once enabled |

**Which I would use first:** `<fill in, in my own words>`

---

## Delete the rule

```powershell
az network nsg rule delete -g rg-intern-lab --nsg-name vm-lab01NSG -n BAD-ssh-any
az network nsg rule list -g rg-intern-lab --nsg-name vm-lab01NSG -o table
```

The second command should print nothing, which confirms the rule is gone.

---

## What I learned

- An NSG rule that allows SSH or RDP from `*` is the most common finding in real reviews.
- The rule is only harmless in my lab because the VM has no public IP. If a public IP were added later, the rule would expose the VM.
- The same finding shows up through three tools that differ in speed, coverage and effort.
- Defender's free tier shows the recommendation, but detail columns need a paid plan.
- Never leave a deliberately bad rule in place overnight.

